Subject: [PATCH] META: raise Linux-Maximum to 7.1

OpenZFS 2.4.3 declares Linux-Maximum: 7.0, which makes configure refuse to
build against our 7.1.8 kernel:

    *** Cannot build against kernel version 7.1.8-smoothkernel.
    *** The maximum supported kernel version is 7.0.

Upstream raised the same cap to 7.1 on master in PR #18682 ("Linux 7.1 compat:
META"), which changed this one line and nothing else -- no code was required
for 7.1 support. That commit landed 2026-06-17, six days after the 2.4.3 tag,
so it missed the release by timing rather than by readiness.

Verified before carrying: OpenZFS 2.4.3 compiles cleanly against a patched
Linux 7.1.8 tree (143 modules, resulting zfs.ko vermagic 7.1.8-smoothkernel).
The only build warnings are the pre-existing objtool __noreturn annotations on
spl_panic() and luaD_throw(); there are no implicit-declaration or
incompatible-pointer warnings that would indicate real kernel API drift.

Why patch META rather than pass --enable-linux-experimental: the flag works for
a local build but cannot reach the DKMS path. scripts/dkms.mkconf hardcodes the
configure argument list used when zfs-dkms rebuilds on the target machine, and
the only injectable knobs are ICP_ROOT, ZFS_DKMS_ENABLE_DEBUG and
ZFS_DKMS_ENABLE_DEBUGINFO. A SmoothNAS appliance rebuilding zfs-dkms against
7.1.8 headers would therefore hit the 7.0 gate and fail. Patching META makes the
raised cap travel with the packaged source, so it holds on every target rebuild.

Removal condition: drop this patch as soon as we move to an OpenZFS release
whose own META declares Linux-Maximum >= 7.1.

--- a/META
+++ b/META
@@ -6,5 +6,5 @@
 Release-Tags:  relext
 License:       CDDL
 Author:        OpenZFS
-Linux-Maximum: 7.0
+Linux-Maximum: 7.1
 Linux-Minimum: 4.18
